{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/filters-faceted","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceted Filters"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/filters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filters"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read your reactive inputs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" through a getter (for example backing them with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":") when creating the table, so the table sees updates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-filtering-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Filtering Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use global filtering features. Global filtering depends on column filtering, so add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnFilteringFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilteringFeature"},{"type":"text","value":". Adding the global filtering feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side filtering, also set up "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(), // if using client-side filtering\n  // manualFiltering: true, // if using manual server-side filtering\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":" registry above lists only the built-in filter function this table uses. Spreading the entire built-in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":" registry ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns: { ...filterFns }"},{"type":"text","value":") still works, but it puts every built-in filter function in your bundle. Register just the functions you use, or pass a function directly to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" option with no registration at all."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"global-filtering-alpine-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Filtering (Alpine) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filtering comes in 2 flavors: Column Filtering and Global Filtering."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide will focus on global filtering, which is a filter that is applied across all columns."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-vs-server-side-filtering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Filtering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filtering should operate over the same dataset as sorting and pagination. Use client-side filtering when the browser has the complete dataset; use server-side filtering when it has only a page or another subset, unless filtering just the loaded rows is intentional."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for combining data operations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The client-side filtered row model also invokes the page-index auto-reset hook when global filtering inputs change. Whether the page index resets depends on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetPageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"autoResetAll"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualPagination"},{"type":"text","value":" options. If filtering is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a global filter state change does not invoke that hook, so reset server-side pagination in the filter change handler when needed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"manual-server-side-global-filtering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual Server-Side Global Filtering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you have decided that you need to implement server-side global filtering instead of using the built-in client-side global filtering, here's how you do that."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" is needed for manual server-side global filtering. Instead, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" that you pass to the table should already be filtered. However, if you have added a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", you can tell the table to skip it by setting the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manualFiltering"},{"type":"text","value":" option to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createTable,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n  manualFiltering: true,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: When using manual global filtering, many of the options that are discussed in the rest of this guide will have no effect. When manualFiltering is set to true, the table instance will not apply any global filtering logic to the rows that are passed to it. Instead, it will assume that the rows are already filtered and will use the data that you pass to it as-is."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"client-side-global-filtering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-Side Global Filtering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using the built-in client-side global filtering, add the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilteringFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" (along with its required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnFilteringFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" prerequisite) and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" factory to your features:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-filter-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Filter Function"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" option sets the filter function used for global filtering. The filter function can be a string that references a filter function (built-in or custom) registered in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":" slot on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", or a filter function passed directly."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n  globalFilterFn: 'includesString', // built-in filter function\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default there are 12 built-in filter functions to choose from:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includesString"},{"type":"text","value":" - Case-insensitive string inclusion"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includesStringSensitive"},{"type":"text","value":" - Case-sensitive string inclusion"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"equalsString"},{"type":"text","value":" - Case-insensitive string equality"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"equals"},{"type":"text","value":" - Strict equality "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"==="}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"weakEquals"},{"type":"text","value":" - Weak equality "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"=="}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrIncludes"},{"type":"text","value":" - The row's array (or string) value includes at least one of the filter values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrIncludesAll"},{"type":"text","value":" - The row's array value includes every filter value"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrIncludesSome"},{"type":"text","value":" - The row's array value includes at least one of the filter values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrHas"},{"type":"text","value":" - The row's scalar value equals at least one of the filter values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inNumberRange"},{"type":"text","value":" - Inclusive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[min, max]"},{"type":"text","value":" number range (endpoints normalized and swapped if reversed)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"between"},{"type":"text","value":" - Exclusive min/max range (blank endpoints are open-ended)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"betweenInclusive"},{"type":"text","value":" - Inclusive min/max range (blank endpoints are open-ended)"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can also define your own custom global filter function and pass it directly to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" table option, as shown "},{"type":"link","href":"#custom-global-filter-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"below"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-filter-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Filter State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" state slice holds the current global filter value, usually a search string (the slice is typed as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"any"},{"type":"text","value":" so custom global filter functions can accept other value shapes). The table's state atoms are reactive in Alpine. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.globalFilter.get()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a reactive read when used inside an Alpine binding ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-text"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":":value"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-if"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-for"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-effect"},{"type":"text","value":", or a getter/method on your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.data"},{"type":"text","value":" object); in event handlers and other untracked code, the same call simply returns the current value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need access to the global filter state outside of the table, you can own the slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" table option. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/store"},{"type":"text","value":" is already a dependency of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/alpine-table"},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAtom"},{"type":"text","value":" is available. The filter value can be read, written, or subscribed to elsewhere (such as in a query key for server-side filtering) without making the table depend on component-local state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\n\nconst globalFilterAtom = createAtom<string>('')\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nglobalFilterAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to global filter changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  atoms: {\n    globalFilter: globalFilterAtom, // table.setGlobalFilter now updates globalFilterAtom\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.globalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onGlobalFilterChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported by owning the slice in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":". It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const local = Alpine.reactive({ globalFilter: '' })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  state: {\n    get globalFilter() {\n      return local.globalFilter // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onGlobalFilterChange: (updater) => {\n    local.globalFilter =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.globalFilter) : updater\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"adding-global-filter-input-to-ui","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adding global filter input to UI"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack table will not add a global filter input UI to your table. You should manually add it to your UI to allow users to filter the table. For example, you can add an input UI above the table to allow users to enter a search term. Bind the input's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":":value"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.globalFilter.get()"},{"type":"text","value":" (a reactive read inside the binding) and update it from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@input"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setGlobalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":". Put interactivity on real elements, not inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<input\n  type=\"text\"\n  placeholder=\"Search all columns...\"\n  :value=\"table.atoms.globalFilter.get() ?? ''\"\n  @input=\"table.setGlobalFilter($event.target.value)\"\n/>","lang":"html"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"custom-global-filter-function","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Global Filter Function"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to use a custom global filter function, you can define the function and pass it to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is often a popular idea to use fuzzy filtering functions for global filtering. This is discussed in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./fuzzy-filtering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fuzzy Filtering Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const customFilterFn = (row, columnId, filterValue) => {\n  return // true if the row should be included in the filtered rows\n}\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  globalFilterFn: customFilterFn,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"initial-global-filter-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial Global Filter State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to set an initial global filter state when the table is initialized, you can pass the global filter state as part of the table "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" option. However, if you are controlling the slice yourself, set the starting value on your external atom or reactive state instead."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  initialState: {\n    globalFilter: 'search term', // if not controlling globalFilter state, set initial state here\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not use both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.globalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" and a controlled "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" (via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":") at the same time, as the controlled value will override "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.globalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"disable-global-filtering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disable Global Filtering"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, global filtering is enabled for all columns. You can disable the global filtering for all columns by using the enableGlobalFilter table option. You can also turn off both column and global filtering by setting the enableFilters table option to false."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling global filtering will cause the column.getCanGlobalFilter API to return false for that column."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Id',\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableGlobalFilter: false, // disable global filtering for this column\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  columns,\n  enableGlobalFilter: false, // disable global filtering for all columns\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-filter-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Filter APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are several APIs that are useful for hooking up your global filter UI:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setGlobalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" - Set the global filter value. Useful for connecting a search input's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"input"},{"type":"text","value":" handler."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetGlobalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" - Reset the global filter value to its initial state, or clear it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetGlobalFilter(true)"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getGlobalFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" - Returns the filter function currently used for global filtering."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getGlobalAutoFilterFn"},{"type":"text","value":" - Returns the default global filter function (currently "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includesString"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getCanGlobalFilter"},{"type":"text","value":" - Returns whether a column participates in global filtering. Useful for debugging which columns are searched."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"global-filtering-setup","text":"Global Filtering Setup","level":3},{"id":"global-filtering-alpine-guide","text":"Global Filtering (Alpine) Guide","level":2},{"id":"client-side-vs-server-side-filtering","text":"Client-Side vs Server-Side Filtering","level":3},{"id":"manual-server-side-global-filtering","text":"Manual Server-Side Global Filtering","level":3},{"id":"client-side-global-filtering","text":"Client-Side Global Filtering","level":3},{"id":"global-filter-function","text":"Global Filter Function","level":3},{"id":"global-filter-state","text":"Global Filter State","level":3},{"id":"adding-global-filter-input-to-ui","text":"Adding global filter input to UI","level":3},{"id":"custom-global-filter-function","text":"Custom Global Filter Function","level":3},{"id":"initial-global-filter-state","text":"Initial Global Filter State","level":3},{"id":"disable-global-filtering","text":"Disable Global Filtering","level":3},{"id":"global-filter-apis","text":"Global Filter APIs","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:\n\n- [Faceted Filters](../examples/filters-faceted)\n- [Column Filters](../examples/filters)\n\nRead your reactive inputs such as `data` through a getter (for example backing them with `Alpine.reactive`) when creating the table, so the table sees updates.\n\n### Global Filtering Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use global filtering features. Global filtering depends on column filtering, so add `columnFilteringFeature` before `globalFilteringFeature`. Adding the global filtering feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side filtering, also set up `filteredRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(), // if using client-side filtering\n  // manualFiltering: true, // if using manual server-side filtering\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The `filterFns` registry above lists only the built-in filter function this table uses. Spreading the entire built-in `filterFns` registry (`filterFns: { ...filterFns }`) still works, but it puts every built-in filter function in your bundle. Register just the functions you use, or pass a function directly to the `globalFilterFn` option with no registration at all.\n\n## Global Filtering (Alpine) Guide\n\nFiltering comes in 2 flavors: Column Filtering and Global Filtering.\n\nThis guide will focus on global filtering, which is a filter that is applied across all columns.\n\n### Client-Side vs Server-Side Filtering\n\nFiltering should operate over the same dataset as sorting and pagination. Use client-side filtering when the browser has the complete dataset; use server-side filtering when it has only a page or another subset, unless filtering just the loaded rows is intentional.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for combining data operations.\n\nThe client-side filtered row model also invokes the page-index auto-reset hook when global filtering inputs change. Whether the page index resets depends on the `autoResetPageIndex`, `autoResetAll`, and `manualPagination` options. If filtering is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a global filter state change does not invoke that hook, so reset server-side pagination in the filter change handler when needed.\n\n### Manual Server-Side Global Filtering\n\nIf you have decided that you need to implement server-side global filtering instead of using the built-in client-side global filtering, here's how you do that.\n\nNo `filteredRowModel` is needed for manual server-side global filtering. Instead, the `data` that you pass to the table should already be filtered. However, if you have added a `filteredRowModel` to `tableFeatures`, you can tell the table to skip it by setting the `manualFiltering` option to `true`.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createTable,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n  manualFiltering: true,\n})\n```\n\nNote: When using manual global filtering, many of the options that are discussed in the rest of this guide will have no effect. When manualFiltering is set to true, the table instance will not apply any global filtering logic to the rows that are passed to it. Instead, it will assume that the rows are already filtered and will use the data that you pass to it as-is.\n\n### Client-Side Global Filtering\n\nIf you are using the built-in client-side global filtering, add the `globalFilteringFeature` (along with its required `columnFilteringFeature` prerequisite) and the `filteredRowModel` factory to your features:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})\n```\n\n### Global Filter Function\n\nThe `globalFilterFn` option sets the filter function used for global filtering. The filter function can be a string that references a filter function (built-in or custom) registered in the `filterFns` slot on `tableFeatures`, or a filter function passed directly.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n  globalFilterFn: 'includesString', // built-in filter function\n})\n```\n\nBy default there are 12 built-in filter functions to choose from:\n\n- `includesString` - Case-insensitive string inclusion\n- `includesStringSensitive` - Case-sensitive string inclusion\n- `equalsString` - Case-insensitive string equality\n- `equals` - Strict equality `===`\n- `weakEquals` - Weak equality `==`\n- `arrIncludes` - The row's array (or string) value includes at least one of the filter values\n- `arrIncludesAll` - The row's array value includes every filter value\n- `arrIncludesSome` - The row's array value includes at least one of the filter values\n- `arrHas` - The row's scalar value equals at least one of the filter values\n- `inNumberRange` - Inclusive `[min, max]` number range (endpoints normalized and swapped if reversed)\n- `between` - Exclusive min/max range (blank endpoints are open-ended)\n- `betweenInclusive` - Inclusive min/max range (blank endpoints are open-ended)\n\nYou can also define your own custom global filter function and pass it directly to the `globalFilterFn` table option, as shown [below](#custom-global-filter-function).\n\n### Global Filter State\n\nThe `globalFilter` state slice holds the current global filter value, usually a search string (the slice is typed as `any` so custom global filter functions can accept other value shapes). The table's state atoms are reactive in Alpine. `table.atoms.globalFilter.get()` is a reactive read when used inside an Alpine binding (`x-text`, `x-html`, `:value`, `x-if`, `x-for`, `x-effect`, or a getter/method on your `Alpine.data` object); in event handlers and other untracked code, the same call simply returns the current value.\n\nIf you need access to the global filter state outside of the table, you can own the slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. `@tanstack/store` is already a dependency of `@tanstack/alpine-table`, so `createAtom` is available. The filter value can be read, written, or subscribed to elsewhere (such as in a query key for server-side filtering) without making the table depend on component-local state.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\n\nconst globalFilterAtom = createAtom<string>('')\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nglobalFilterAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to global filter changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  atoms: {\n    globalFilter: globalFilterAtom, // table.setGlobalFilter now updates globalFilterAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.globalFilter` plus `onGlobalFilterChange` pattern is still supported by owning the slice in `Alpine.reactive`. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({ globalFilter: '' })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  state: {\n    get globalFilter() {\n      return local.globalFilter // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onGlobalFilterChange: (updater) => {\n    local.globalFilter =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.globalFilter) : updater\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Adding global filter input to UI\n\nTanStack table will not add a global filter input UI to your table. You should manually add it to your UI to allow users to filter the table. For example, you can add an input UI above the table to allow users to enter a search term. Bind the input's `:value` to `table.atoms.globalFilter.get()` (a reactive read inside the binding) and update it from `@input` with `table.setGlobalFilter`. Put interactivity on real elements, not inside `x-html`.\n\n```html\n<input\n  type=\"text\"\n  placeholder=\"Search all columns...\"\n  :value=\"table.atoms.globalFilter.get() ?? ''\"\n  @input=\"table.setGlobalFilter($event.target.value)\"\n/>\n```\n\n### Custom Global Filter Function\n\nIf you want to use a custom global filter function, you can define the function and pass it to the `globalFilterFn` option.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> It is often a popular idea to use fuzzy filtering functions for global filtering. This is discussed in the [Fuzzy Filtering Guide](./fuzzy-filtering).\n\n```ts\nconst customFilterFn = (row, columnId, filterValue) => {\n  return // true if the row should be included in the filtered rows\n}\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  globalFilterFn: customFilterFn,\n})\n```\n\n### Initial Global Filter State\n\nIf you want to set an initial global filter state when the table is initialized, you can pass the global filter state as part of the table `initialState` option. However, if you are controlling the slice yourself, set the starting value on your external atom or reactive state instead.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  initialState: {\n    globalFilter: 'search term', // if not controlling globalFilter state, set initial state here\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not use both `initialState.globalFilter` and a controlled `globalFilter` (via `atoms` or `state`) at the same time, as the controlled value will override `initialState.globalFilter`.\n\n### Disable Global Filtering\n\nBy default, global filtering is enabled for all columns. You can disable the global filtering for all columns by using the enableGlobalFilter table option. You can also turn off both column and global filtering by setting the enableFilters table option to false.\n\nDisabling global filtering will cause the column.getCanGlobalFilter API to return false for that column.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Id',\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableGlobalFilter: false, // disable global filtering for this column\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  columns,\n  enableGlobalFilter: false, // disable global filtering for all columns\n})\n```\n\n### Global Filter APIs\n\nThere are several APIs that are useful for hooking up your global filter UI:\n\n- `table.setGlobalFilter` - Set the global filter value. Useful for connecting a search input's `input` handler.\n- `table.resetGlobalFilter` - Reset the global filter value to its initial state, or clear it with `table.resetGlobalFilter(true)`.\n- `table.getGlobalFilterFn` - Returns the filter function currently used for global filtering.\n- `table.getGlobalAutoFilterFn` - Returns the default global filter function (currently `includesString`).\n- `column.getCanGlobalFilter` - Returns whether a column participates in global filtering. Useful for debugging which columns are searched.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Global Filtering (Alpine) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:\n\n- [Faceted Filters](../examples/filters-faceted)\n- [Column Filters](../examples/filters)\n\nRead your reactive inputs such as `data` through a getter (for example backing them with `Alpine.reactive`) when creating the table, so the table sees updates.\n\n### Global Filtering Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use global filtering features. Global filtering depends on column filtering, so add `columnFilteringFeature` before `globalFilteringFeature`. Adding the global filtering feature enables the related APIs. If you use client-side filtering, also set up `filteredRowModel` after its feature, since row model slots are type-checked.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(), // if using client-side filtering\n  // manualFiltering: true, // if using manual server-side filtering\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The `filterFns` registry above lists only the built-in filter function this table uses. Spreading the entire built-in `filterFns` registry (`filterFns: { ...filterFns }`) still works, but it puts every built-in filter function in your bundle. Register just the functions you use, or pass a function directly to the `globalFilterFn` option with no registration at all.\n\n## Global Filtering (Alpine) Guide\n\nFiltering comes in 2 flavors: Column Filtering and Global Filtering.\n\nThis guide will focus on global filtering, which is a filter that is applied across all columns.\n\n### Client-Side vs Server-Side Filtering\n\nFiltering should operate over the same dataset as sorting and pagination. Use client-side filtering when the browser has the complete dataset; use server-side filtering when it has only a page or another subset, unless filtering just the loaded rows is intentional.\n\nSee the [Client-Side vs Server-Side Guide](../../../guide/client-side-vs-server-side) for the full decision framework, performance factors, and guidance for combining data operations.\n\nThe client-side filtered row model also invokes the page-index auto-reset hook when global filtering inputs change. Whether the page index resets depends on the `autoResetPageIndex`, `autoResetAll`, and `manualPagination` options. If filtering is manual and this row model is omitted or bypassed, a global filter state change does not invoke that hook, so reset server-side pagination in the filter change handler when needed.\n\n### Manual Server-Side Global Filtering\n\nIf you have decided that you need to implement server-side global filtering instead of using the built-in client-side global filtering, here's how you do that.\n\nNo `filteredRowModel` is needed for manual server-side global filtering. Instead, the `data` that you pass to the table should already be filtered. However, if you have added a `filteredRowModel` to `tableFeatures`, you can tell the table to skip it by setting the `manualFiltering` option to `true`.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createTable,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n  manualFiltering: true,\n})\n```\n\nNote: When using manual global filtering, many of the options that are discussed in the rest of this guide will have no effect. When manualFiltering is set to true, the table instance will not apply any global filtering logic to the rows that are passed to it. Instead, it will assume that the rows are already filtered and will use the data that you pass to it as-is.\n\n### Client-Side Global Filtering\n\nIf you are using the built-in client-side global filtering, add the `globalFilteringFeature` (along with its required `columnFilteringFeature` prerequisite) and the `filteredRowModel` factory to your features:\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  globalFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n})\n```\n\n### Global Filter Function\n\nThe `globalFilterFn` option sets the filter function used for global filtering. The filter function can be a string that references a filter function (built-in or custom) registered in the `filterFns` slot on `tableFeatures`, or a filter function passed directly.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n  globalFilterFn: 'includesString', // built-in filter function\n})\n```\n\nBy default there are 12 built-in filter functions to choose from:\n\n- `includesString` - Case-insensitive string inclusion\n- `includesStringSensitive` - Case-sensitive string inclusion\n- `equalsString` - Case-insensitive string equality\n- `equals` - Strict equality `===`\n- `weakEquals` - Weak equality `==`\n- `arrIncludes` - The row's array (or string) value includes at least one of the filter values\n- `arrIncludesAll` - The row's array value includes every filter value\n- `arrIncludesSome` - The row's array value includes at least one of the filter values\n- `arrHas` - The row's scalar value equals at least one of the filter values\n- `inNumberRange` - Inclusive `[min, max]` number range (endpoints normalized and swapped if reversed)\n- `between` - Exclusive min/max range (blank endpoints are open-ended)\n- `betweenInclusive` - Inclusive min/max range (blank endpoints are open-ended)\n\nYou can also define your own custom global filter function and pass it directly to the `globalFilterFn` table option, as shown [below](#custom-global-filter-function).\n\n### Global Filter State\n\nThe `globalFilter` state slice holds the current global filter value, usually a search string (the slice is typed as `any` so custom global filter functions can accept other value shapes). The table's state atoms are reactive in Alpine. `table.atoms.globalFilter.get()` is a reactive read when used inside an Alpine binding (`x-text`, `x-html`, `:value`, `x-if`, `x-for`, `x-effect`, or a getter/method on your `Alpine.data` object); in event handlers and other untracked code, the same call simply returns the current value.\n\nIf you need access to the global filter state outside of the table, you can own the slice yourself. The recommended way in v9 is an external atom passed through the `atoms` table option. `@tanstack/store` is already a dependency of `@tanstack/alpine-table`, so `createAtom` is available. The filter value can be read, written, or subscribed to elsewhere (such as in a query key for server-side filtering) without making the table depend on component-local state.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\n\nconst globalFilterAtom = createAtom<string>('')\n\n// subscribe to the atom wherever you need the value (e.g. for a query key)\nglobalFilterAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to global filter changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  atoms: {\n    globalFilter: globalFilterAtom, // table.setGlobalFilter now updates globalFilterAtom\n  },\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.globalFilter` plus `onGlobalFilterChange` pattern is still supported by owning the slice in `Alpine.reactive`. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({ globalFilter: '' })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  state: {\n    get globalFilter() {\n      return local.globalFilter // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n  },\n  onGlobalFilterChange: (updater) => {\n    local.globalFilter =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.globalFilter) : updater\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Adding global filter input to UI\n\nTanStack table will not add a global filter input UI to your table. You should manually add it to your UI to allow users to filter the table. For example, you can add an input UI above the table to allow users to enter a search term. Bind the input's `:value` to `table.atoms.globalFilter.get()` (a reactive read inside the binding) and update it from `@input` with `table.setGlobalFilter`. Put interactivity on real elements, not inside `x-html`.\n\n```html\n<input\n  type=\"text\"\n  placeholder=\"Search all columns...\"\n  :value=\"table.atoms.globalFilter.get() ?? ''\"\n  @input=\"table.setGlobalFilter($event.target.value)\"\n/>\n```\n\n### Custom Global Filter Function\n\nIf you want to use a custom global filter function, you can define the function and pass it to the `globalFilterFn` option.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> It is often a popular idea to use fuzzy filtering functions for global filtering. This is discussed in the [Fuzzy Filtering Guide](./fuzzy-filtering).\n\n```ts\nconst customFilterFn = (row, columnId, filterValue) => {\n  return // true if the row should be included in the filtered rows\n}\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  globalFilterFn: customFilterFn,\n})\n```\n\n### Initial Global Filter State\n\nIf you want to set an initial global filter state when the table is initialized, you can pass the global filter state as part of the table `initialState` option. However, if you are controlling the slice yourself, set the starting value on your external atom or reactive state instead.\n\n```ts\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  initialState: {\n    globalFilter: 'search term', // if not controlling globalFilter state, set initial state here\n  },\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Do not use both `initialState.globalFilter` and a controlled `globalFilter` (via `atoms` or `state`) at the same time, as the controlled value will override `initialState.globalFilter`.\n\n### Disable Global Filtering\n\nBy default, global filtering is enabled for all columns. You can disable the global filtering for all columns by using the enableGlobalFilter table option. You can also turn off both column and global filtering by setting the enableFilters table option to false.\n\nDisabling global filtering will cause the column.getCanGlobalFilter API to return false for that column.\n\n```ts\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    header: () => 'Id',\n    accessorKey: 'id',\n    enableGlobalFilter: false, // disable global filtering for this column\n  },\n  //...\n]\n//...\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  // other options...\n  columns,\n  enableGlobalFilter: false, // disable global filtering for all columns\n})\n```\n\n### Global Filter APIs\n\nThere are several APIs that are useful for hooking up your global filter UI:\n\n- `table.setGlobalFilter` - Set the global filter value. Useful for connecting a search input's `input` handler.\n- `table.resetGlobalFilter` - Reset the global filter value to its initial state, or clear it with `table.resetGlobalFilter(true)`.\n- `table.getGlobalFilterFn` - Returns the filter function currently used for global filtering.\n- `table.getGlobalAutoFilterFn` - Returns the default global filter function (currently `includesString`).\n- `column.getCanGlobalFilter` - Returns whether a column participates in global filtering. Useful for debugging which columns are searched.\n"}
